WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:03.540
In this part of our optimization tools,

2
00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:06.259
I'd like to talk about scalability settings

3
00:00:06.259 --> 00:00:09.339
as they allow you to adjust the rendering

4
00:00:09.339 --> 00:00:12.220
demands of any scene quickly and easily inside

5
00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:13.300
of the editor.

6
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.900
We can also use scalability commands

7
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.899
to adjust how our project is going

8
00:00:17.899 --> 00:00:22.059
to look in a settings menu in the final product

9
00:00:22.059 --> 00:00:25.539
once it's been cooked and ready for a user to utilize.

10
00:00:25.539 --> 00:00:28.059
Now, what scalability does is, again,

11
00:00:28.059 --> 00:00:31.219
it adjusts how things are going to be rendered.

12
00:00:31.219 --> 00:00:33.179
So in the example in front of us, we

13
00:00:33.179 --> 00:00:35.780
can see the various default scalability settings

14
00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.700
taken right from the editor.

15
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:43.340
And if we were to click on, say, Near or Low or Medium,

16
00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:46.640
we are going to reduce the settings that

17
00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:48.039
are used or the rendering demands

18
00:00:48.039 --> 00:00:49.359
for that particular category.

19
00:00:49.359 --> 00:00:53.000
For example, if we adjust our view distance to Near,

20
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.039
we are going to reduce the amount of objects

21
00:00:56.039 --> 00:00:59.759
that are going to be rendered by pulling the culling

22
00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:02.560
or telling the renderer to stop rendering objects

23
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.439
past a certain distance closer to the camera.

24
00:01:05.439 --> 00:01:08.040
Now, the exact opposite of this is true.

25
00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.280
If we were to go from, say, Near to Epic or even

26
00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.079
Cinematic, we would be increasing that distance

27
00:01:14.079 --> 00:01:17.040
or increasing that rendering parameter.

28
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.879
Now, this does have quite a significant effect

29
00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:21.680
on the overall look of your scene.

30
00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:24.640
Here we have an example taken from the Electric Dreams demo

31
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.680
that shows Low, Medium, High, Epic, and Cinematic all taken

32
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:29.760
from the same spot.

33
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.340
And we can really see the difference

34
00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:33.519
between Low and Cinematic.

35
00:01:33.519 --> 00:01:35.959
There is just no shadows in Low.

36
00:01:35.959 --> 00:01:40.560
There is no subsurface scattering.

37
00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.680
It's a very low-end looking scene.

38
00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.920
And then as we start to get to Medium, High, Epic,

39
00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:50.250
and Cinematic, we're really starting to turn up the details

40
00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:53.049
and increase the overall quality of the scene.

41
00:01:53.049 --> 00:01:55.129
Now, there's a tool that we can use

42
00:01:55.129 --> 00:01:58.409
to help us further define what is set inside

43
00:01:58.409 --> 00:01:59.469
of these settings.

44
00:01:59.469 --> 00:02:03.209
And we do that using something called the Device Profiles

45
00:02:03.209 --> 00:02:03.769
tool.

46
00:02:03.769 --> 00:02:06.409
Now, the Device Profiles tool allows

47
00:02:06.409 --> 00:02:12.009
us to specify how our project should look when run on a wide

48
00:02:12.009 --> 00:02:13.610
range of different devices.

49
00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:16.810
Now, to utilize this, we're going to open it up inside

50
00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:20.110
of our Unreal Editor session as a separate tool.

51
00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:22.889
And then all we need to do to activate each profile

52
00:02:22.889 --> 00:02:24.930
is click on the three little ellipses

53
00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:28.030
next to the name of the profile we wish to activate.

54
00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:29.409
So let's pop over to the editor

55
00:02:29.409 --> 00:02:30.889
and look at how we do this.

56
00:02:30.889 --> 00:02:33.449
So we're going to come up here to our tools.

57
00:02:33.449 --> 00:02:35.169
And then under Platforms, we're

58
00:02:35.169 --> 00:02:38.590
going to have our Device Profiles.

59
00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:42.789
So right here is our Device Profiles.

60
00:02:42.789 --> 00:02:45.830
And again, if we want to grab our Windows Editor,

61
00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:47.770
I'm just going to hit these three little ellipses right

62
00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:48.270
here.

63
00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:50.789
And this is going to bring up the profiles for this.

64
00:02:50.789 --> 00:02:53.069
So you can see here, we have a bunch of different things

65
00:02:53.069 --> 00:02:54.030
that we can do.

66
00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:56.909
Underneath Rendering, we can add rendering commands

67
00:02:56.909 --> 00:03:00.590
like maybe allow HDR or allow static lighting.

68
00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:02.990
And once we put one of those in there,

69
00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:04.349
I'm just going to expand this.

70
00:03:04.349 --> 00:03:06.490
You can see here, we can allow static lighting.

71
00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:08.069
And if we hover over this, it actually

72
00:03:08.069 --> 00:03:11.800
is also going to give us a little description of what

73
00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:15.400
this command does and the inputs it requires.

74
00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.599
We can also do this for our scalability group.

75
00:03:17.599 --> 00:03:20.520
You can see here, we have anti-aliasing.

76
00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:24.199
We have quality number of levels for our effects,

77
00:03:24.199 --> 00:03:26.479
our foliage, a whole bunch of other options.

78
00:03:26.479 --> 00:03:29.719
And again, I can do this if I just close that down here.

79
00:03:29.719 --> 00:03:31.080
I can do this for everything.

80
00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.520
So here is something for iOS.

81
00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:35.840
If I scroll down this list further,

82
00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.159
you're going to see there's a bunch of iPhones in here,

83
00:03:38.159 --> 00:03:39.039
iPads, Mac TV.

84
00:03:39.039 --> 00:03:40.599
There's all types of Linux stuff.

85
00:03:40.599 --> 00:03:44.199
There's Android, all types of different things inside of here

86
00:03:44.199 --> 00:03:47.199
that we can set these device profiles to.

87
00:03:47.199 --> 00:03:49.919
And again, this allows us to adjust

88
00:03:49.919 --> 00:03:51.919
how our project is going to look,

89
00:03:51.919 --> 00:03:53.960
not only inside of the editor, but also

90
00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:59.400
on the wide range of devices it could be published for.
